Ladybird keeps a disc, circle or square marker as generated text (its
symbol and a space), and now ends an outside one 5px further from its
list item. Blink and WebKit draw these bullets as shapes instead, with
a gap that Blink’s ListMarker and WebKit’s RenderListOutsideMarker::
updateInlineMargins() derive from the font’s integer ascent A: the gap
is T + 7 - D, where T = floor(2A/3) and D = floor((T + 1)/2). Gecko
keeps them as text, but sets them in its bundled -moz-bullet-font, in
which a symbol and a space advance about 0.51em. Measured on this Mac
from the marker’s right edge to the text, at 16px Times, 19px Arial
and 32px Helvetica, then circle and square at 19px Arial: Chrome 158
gave 13, 14, 19, 14 and 14px, Firefox 151 gave 10, 12, 18, 16 and 12px,
and Ladybird had 7, 8, 13, 8 and 9px, and now has 12, 13, 18, 13 and
14px. Chrome’s gap was 6px ahead of Ladybird’s at every size, since
both grow with the font, and Firefox’s was 3px to 5px ahead for discs
and squares (8px for circles), so a fixed 5px lands between the two.
Decimal markers already matched at 9px in all three, and keep their
place. The bullets themselves stay a little smaller than the other
browsers’, at the size of the font’s own U+2022 glyph; matching that
would take a bullet that doesn’t come from the page’s font.
